    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Angela presents to the resuscitation bay with fever, acute confusion, and dyspnea. Physical examination reveals multiple petechiae on the oral mucosa, a new early diastolic murmur, and splinter hemorrhages under the fingernails. Blood cultures are drawn. What is the most appropriate empiric antimicrobial regimen while awaiting culture results?', '["Intravenous Piperacillin-Tazobactam as monotherapy", "Intravenous Vancomycin and Ceftriaxone", "Intravenous Azithromycin and Ciprofloxacin", "Oral Amoxicillin and Clindamycin", "Intravenous Acyclovir and Ganciclovir"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical presentation is highly suggestive of acute infective endocarditis. Empiric antibiotic therapy must be initiated promptly in patients with suspected IE who are acutely ill or hemodynamically unstable, after drawing at least 2-3 sets of blood cultures. The standard empiric regimen for acute IE (targeting MRSA, streptococci, and enterococci) consists of intravenous Vancomycin to cover Gram-positives including resistant strains, combined with intravenous Ceftriaxone (2 g every 12 hours) or Gentamicin. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 55; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old male named David presents to the high-acuity area with fever, acute confusion, and dyspnea. Physical examination reveals multiple petechiae on the oral mucosa, a new early diastolic murmur, and splinter hemorrhages under the fingernails. Blood cultures are drawn. What is the most appropriate empiric antimicrobial regimen while awaiting culture results?', '["Oral Amoxicillin and Clindamycin", "Intravenous Piperacillin-Tazobactam as monotherapy", "Intravenous Acyclovir and Ganciclovir", "Intravenous Vancomycin and Ceftriaxone", "Intravenous Azithromycin and Ciprofloxacin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical presentation is highly suggestive of acute infective endocarditis. Empiric antibiotic therapy must be initiated promptly in patients with suspected IE who are acutely ill or hemodynamically unstable, after drawing at least 2-3 sets of blood cultures. The standard empiric regimen for acute IE (targeting MRSA, streptococci, and enterococci) consists of intravenous Vancomycin to cover Gram-positives including resistant strains, combined with intravenous Ceftriaxone (2 g every 12 hours) or Gentamicin. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 55; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Kimberly is brought to the Emergency Department after a transient loss of consciousness. The episode occurred suddenly while she was sitting in a chair, with no prodromal symptoms, and was witnessed by a co-worker who reports the patient was unresponsive for 30 seconds. Which of the following features in this patient''s presentation represents a high-risk factor that mandates admission and cardiac telemetry monitoring?', '["Loss of consciousness immediately following a painful stimulus", "Sudden loss of consciousness without prodrome while in a seated position", "Age younger than 40 years old and a history of motion sickness", "Loss of consciousness occurring immediately upon standing up", "The presence of lightheadedness and nausea prior to losing consciousness"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Syncope must be risk-stratified to differentiate benign causes from life-threatening cardiogenic syncope (arrhythmia, structural heart disease). High-risk features indicating cardiogenic syncope include: sudden onset without prodrome, syncope occurring while supine or seated, syncope during exertion, family history of sudden cardiac death, or abnormal ECG. These patients mandate admission for continuous telemetry monitoring. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 52; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 6)'),
