    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old female named Betty is brought to the Emergency Department after a transient loss of consciousness. The episode occurred suddenly while she was sitting in a chair, with no prodromal symptoms, and was witnessed by a co-worker who reports the patient was unresponsive for 30 seconds. Which of the following features in this patient''s presentation represents a high-risk factor that mandates admission and cardiac telemetry monitoring?', '["Sudden loss of consciousness without prodrome while in a seated position", "Age younger than 40 years old and a history of motion sickness", "The presence of lightheadedness and nausea prior to losing consciousness", "Loss of consciousness occurring immediately upon standing up", "Loss of consciousness immediately following a painful stimulus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Syncope must be risk-stratified to differentiate benign causes from life-threatening cardiogenic syncope (arrhythmia, structural heart disease). High-risk features indicating cardiogenic syncope include: sudden onset without prodrome, syncope occurring while supine or seated, syncope during exertion, family history of sudden cardiac death, or abnormal ECG. These patients mandate admission for continuous telemetry monitoring. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 52; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 6)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old male named Jeffrey is brought to the Emergency Department after a transient loss of consciousness. The episode occurred suddenly while he was sitting in a chair, with no prodromal symptoms, and was witnessed by a co-worker who reports the patient was unresponsive for 30 seconds. Which of the following features in this patient''s presentation represents a high-risk factor that mandates admission and cardiac telemetry monitoring?', '["Sudden loss of consciousness without prodrome while in a seated position", "The presence of lightheadedness and nausea prior to losing consciousness", "Loss of consciousness occurring immediately upon standing up", "Loss of consciousness immediately following a painful stimulus", "Age younger than 40 years old and a history of motion sickness"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Syncope must be risk-stratified to differentiate benign causes from life-threatening cardiogenic syncope (arrhythmia, structural heart disease). High-risk features indicating cardiogenic syncope include: sudden onset without prodrome, syncope occurring while supine or seated, syncope during exertion, family history of sudden cardiac death, or abnormal ECG. These patients mandate admission for continuous telemetry monitoring. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 52; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 6)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Mark presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 88/54 mmHg, HR 114 bpm, RR 32 rpm, and SpO2 85% on room air. The physical exam reveals a diaphoretic patient with prominent jugular venous distension. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 114 bpm with an S1Q3T3 pattern. A bedside echocardiogram reveals right ventricular dilation and hypokinesis of the RV free wall (McConnell''s sign). Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Initiation of therapeutic anticoagulation with low-molecular-weight heparin", "Discharge home on oral apixaban with close outpatient follow-up", "Intravenous thrombolysis (e.g., Alteplase) or catheter-directed embolectomy", "Urgent coronary angiography to evaluate for acute myocardial infarction", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg and a nitroglycerin infusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient is presenting with a massive pulmonary embolism (PE), defined by acute PE causing hemodynamic instability (systolic blood pressure <90 mmHg). The presence of right ventricular strain (evidenced by McConnell''s sign on echo, S1Q3T3 pattern on ECG, and JVD) confirms significant right heart overload. The treatment of choice for massive PE with persistent shock is intravenous thrombolysis (systemic Alteplase 100 mg) to rapidly dissolve the clot and restore pulmonary perfusion. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 56; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 7)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female named Elizabeth presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 80/56 mmHg, HR 120 bpm, RR 22 rpm, and SpO2 92% on room air. The physical exam reveals a diaphoretic patient with prominent jugular venous distension. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 120 bpm with an S1Q3T3 pattern. A bedside echocardiogram reveals right ventricular dilation and hypokinesis of the RV free wall (McConnell''s sign). Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Discharge home on oral apixaban with close outpatient follow-up", "Urgent coronary angiography to evaluate for acute myocardial infarction", "Initiation of therapeutic anticoagulation with low-molecular-weight heparin", "Intravenous thrombolysis (e.g., Alteplase) or catheter-directed embolectomy", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg and a nitroglycerin infusion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is presenting with a massive pulmonary embolism (PE), defined by acute PE causing hemodynamic instability (systolic blood pressure <90 mmHg). The presence of right ventricular strain (evidenced by McConnell''s sign on echo, S1Q3T3 pattern on ECG, and JVD) confirms significant right heart overload. The treatment of choice for massive PE with persistent shock is intravenous thrombolysis (systemic Alteplase 100 mg) to rapidly dissolve the clot and restore pulmonary perfusion. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 56; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 7)'),
