    (v_topic_id, 'A 46-year-old male named Gary presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 80/54 mmHg, HR 108 bpm, RR 30 rpm, and SpO2 87% on room air. The physical exam reveals a diaphoretic patient with prominent jugular venous distension. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 108 bpm with an S1Q3T3 pattern. A bedside echocardiogram reveals right ventricular dilation and hypokinesis of the RV free wall (McConnell''s sign). Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Discharge home on oral apixaban with close outpatient follow-up", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg and a nitroglycerin infusion", "Initiation of therapeutic anticoagulation with low-molecular-weight heparin", "Urgent coronary angiography to evaluate for acute myocardial infarction", "Intravenous thrombolysis (e.g., Alteplase) or catheter-directed embolectomy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient is presenting with a massive pulmonary embolism (PE), defined by acute PE causing hemodynamic instability (systolic blood pressure <90 mmHg). The presence of right ventricular strain (evidenced by McConnell''s sign on echo, S1Q3T3 pattern on ECG, and JVD) confirms significant right heart overload. The treatment of choice for massive PE with persistent shock is intravenous thrombolysis (systemic Alteplase 100 mg) to rapidly dissolve the clot and restore pulmonary perfusion. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 56; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 7)'),
