    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old female named Jessica presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and near-syncope. Vitals are BP 92/48 mmHg, HR 132 bpm, RR 26 rpm, and SpO2 90% on room air. The physical exam reveals a diaphoretic patient with prominent jugular venous distension. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 132 bpm with an S1Q3T3 pattern. A bedside echocardiogram reveals right ventricular dilation and hypokinesis of the RV free wall (McConnell''s sign). Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Intravenous thrombolysis (e.g., Alteplase) or catheter-directed embolectomy", "Discharge home on oral apixaban with close outpatient follow-up", "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg and a nitroglycerin infusion", "Urgent coronary angiography to evaluate for acute myocardial infarction", "Initiation of therapeutic anticoagulation with low-molecular-weight heparin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is presenting with a massive pulmonary embolism (PE), defined by acute PE causing hemodynamic instability (systolic blood pressure <90 mmHg). The presence of right ventricular strain (evidenced by McConnell''s sign on echo, S1Q3T3 pattern on ECG, and JVD) confirms significant right heart overload. The treatment of choice for massive PE with persistent shock is intravenous thrombolysis (systemic Alteplase 100 mg) to rapidly dissolve the clot and restore pulmonary perfusion. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 56; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 7)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Elizabeth goes into cardiac arrest in the Emergency Department. CPR is ongoing. The cardiac monitor shows a slow, wide-complex rhythm at 44 bpm. There is no palpable carotid pulse. Which of the following represents the correct immediate action according to ACLS protocols?', '["Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Continue high-quality CPR, administer Epinephrine 1 mg IV push every 3\u20135 minutes, and search for reversible causes (H''s and T''s)", "Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately to override the slow rhythm", "Administer a rapid bolus of Amiodarone 300 mg intravenously", "Perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules biphasic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cardiac arrest rhythm is Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA). The ACLS algorithm for non-shockable rhythms (PEA and Asystole) dictates: (1) High-quality CPR continuously; (2) Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO as soon as possible, repeated every 3–5 minutes; (3) Search for and treat reversible causes (the 5 H''s and 5 T''s: Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion acidosis, Hypo-/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary, Thrombosis coronary). Defibrillation or cardioversion is NOT indicated for PEA. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-year-old male named Steven goes into cardiac arrest in the Emergency Department. CPR is ongoing. The cardiac monitor shows a slow, wide-complex rhythm at 44 bpm. There is no palpable carotid pulse. Which of the following represents the correct immediate action according to ACLS protocols?', '["Perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules biphasic", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Administer a rapid bolus of Amiodarone 300 mg intravenously", "Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately to override the slow rhythm", "Continue high-quality CPR, administer Epinephrine 1 mg IV push every 3\u20135 minutes, and search for reversible causes (H''s and T''s)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The cardiac arrest rhythm is Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA). The ACLS algorithm for non-shockable rhythms (PEA and Asystole) dictates: (1) High-quality CPR continuously; (2) Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO as soon as possible, repeated every 3–5 minutes; (3) Search for and treat reversible causes (the 5 H''s and 5 T''s: Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion acidosis, Hypo-/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary, Thrombosis coronary). Defibrillation or cardioversion is NOT indicated for PEA. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
