    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Jonathan goes into cardiac arrest in the Emergency Department. CPR is ongoing. The cardiac monitor shows a slow, wide-complex rhythm at 42 bpm. There is no palpable carotid pulse. Which of the following represents the correct immediate action according to ACLS protocols?', '["Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately to override the slow rhythm", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Continue high-quality CPR, administer Epinephrine 1 mg IV push every 3\u20135 minutes, and search for reversible causes (H''s and T''s)", "Perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules biphasic", "Administer a rapid bolus of Amiodarone 300 mg intravenously"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cardiac arrest rhythm is Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA). The ACLS algorithm for non-shockable rhythms (PEA and Asystole) dictates: (1) High-quality CPR continuously; (2) Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO as soon as possible, repeated every 3–5 minutes; (3) Search for and treat reversible causes (the 5 H''s and 5 T''s: Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion acidosis, Hypo-/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary, Thrombosis coronary). Defibrillation or cardioversion is NOT indicated for PEA. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old female named Emily goes into cardiac arrest in the Emergency Department. CPR is ongoing. The cardiac monitor shows a slow, wide-complex rhythm at 32 bpm. There is no palpable carotid pulse. Which of the following represents the correct immediate action according to ACLS protocols?', '["Administer a rapid bolus of Amiodarone 300 mg intravenously", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules biphasic", "Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately to override the slow rhythm", "Continue high-quality CPR, administer Epinephrine 1 mg IV push every 3\u20135 minutes, and search for reversible causes (H''s and T''s)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The cardiac arrest rhythm is Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA). The ACLS algorithm for non-shockable rhythms (PEA and Asystole) dictates: (1) High-quality CPR continuously; (2) Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO as soon as possible, repeated every 3–5 minutes; (3) Search for and treat reversible causes (the 5 H''s and 5 T''s: Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion acidosis, Hypo-/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary, Thrombosis coronary). Defibrillation or cardioversion is NOT indicated for PEA. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old male named Jeffrey with a past medical history of a permanent pacemaker presents with severe lightheadedness and dyspnea. Vitals are BP 88/60 mmHg, HR 35 bpm. The cardiac monitor shows a ventricular rate of 35 bpm. The ECG demonstrates occasional pacemaker spikes that are not followed by a QRS complex. Which of the following represents the most appropriate immediate Emergency Department intervention?', '["Immediately arrange for emergent surgical explantation of the pacemaker leads", "Initiate an emergency infusion of norepinephrine at 10 mcg/min to stimulate intrinsic pacing", "Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately and place a magnet over the pacemaker generator to revert it to asynchronous pacing mode", "Administer a bolus of 1 liter of normal saline and repeat the ECG in 1 hour", "Administer amiodarone 150 mg IV over 10 minutes to stabilize the pacemaker-myocardium interface"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient is experiencing pacemaker malfunction presenting as failure to capture (pacemaker spikes are visible but do not depolarize the ventricles, resulting in symptomatic bradycardia). The immediate management for symptomatic bradycardia due to pacemaker failure is transcutaneous pacing (TCP) to restore rate and perfusion. Additionally, placing a magnet over the pacemaker generator causes the device to revert to its default asynchronous pacing mode (often VOO or DOO), which can override sensing errors or software issues. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 58 & 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old female named Jessica with a past medical history of a permanent pacemaker presents with severe lightheadedness and dyspnea. Vitals are BP 82/50 mmHg, HR 44 bpm. The cardiac monitor shows a ventricular rate of 44 bpm. The ECG demonstrates occasional pacemaker spikes that are not followed by a QRS complex. Which of the following represents the most appropriate immediate Emergency Department intervention?', '["Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately and place a magnet over the pacemaker generator to revert it to asynchronous pacing mode", "Administer a bolus of 1 liter of normal saline and repeat the ECG in 1 hour", "Initiate an emergency infusion of norepinephrine at 10 mcg/min to stimulate intrinsic pacing", "Administer amiodarone 150 mg IV over 10 minutes to stabilize the pacemaker-myocardium interface", "Immediately arrange for emergent surgical explantation of the pacemaker leads"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient is experiencing pacemaker malfunction presenting as failure to capture (pacemaker spikes are visible but do not depolarize the ventricles, resulting in symptomatic bradycardia). The immediate management for symptomatic bradycardia due to pacemaker failure is transcutaneous pacing (TCP) to restore rate and perfusion. Additionally, placing a magnet over the pacemaker generator causes the device to revert to its default asynchronous pacing mode (often VOO or DOO), which can override sensing errors or software issues. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 58 & 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
