    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Thomas with a past medical history of a permanent pacemaker presents with severe lightheadedness and dyspnea. Vitals are BP 80/60 mmHg, HR 35 bpm. The cardiac monitor shows a ventricular rate of 35 bpm. The ECG demonstrates occasional pacemaker spikes that are not followed by a QRS complex. Which of the following represents the most appropriate immediate Emergency Department intervention?', '["Immediately arrange for emergent surgical explantation of the pacemaker leads", "Initiate transcutaneous pacing immediately and place a magnet over the pacemaker generator to revert it to asynchronous pacing mode", "Administer a bolus of 1 liter of normal saline and repeat the ECG in 1 hour", "Administer amiodarone 150 mg IV over 10 minutes to stabilize the pacemaker-myocardium interface", "Initiate an emergency infusion of norepinephrine at 10 mcg/min to stimulate intrinsic pacing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient is experiencing pacemaker malfunction presenting as failure to capture (pacemaker spikes are visible but do not depolarize the ventricles, resulting in symptomatic bradycardia). The immediate management for symptomatic bradycardia due to pacemaker failure is transcutaneous pacing (TCP) to restore rate and perfusion. Additionally, placing a magnet over the pacemaker generator causes the device to revert to its default asynchronous pacing mode (often VOO or DOO), which can override sensing errors or software issues. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 58 & 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
